All temporal objectshave their ownadd() method:

All temporal objectshave their ownsubtract() method:

Theadd()method accepts aduration objectas input.

Example:{ days: 10 }.

It returns a new temporal date object moved forward by the duration.

Thesubtract()method accepts aduration objectas input.

Example:{ days: 10 }.

It returns a new temporal date object moved backward by the duration.

Both add and subtracthandle date boundaries:

Adding one day to March 31st is April 1st.

Temporal automatically handles different month lengths.

Adding years works correctly, even for leap years.

Theadd()andsubtract()methods accept aduration objectas input.

Example:{ months: 2, days: 7, hours: 1 }.

The followingduration unitsare supported:

You can safely add or subtract time.

The original value does not change.

From aTemporal.Instantyou can only add or subtract time durations
(hours, minutes, seconds) but not calendar durations like months or years,
as their length can vary depending on the time zone and the calendar.

ZonedDateTime handles daylight saving time (DST) safely.

If a DST change occurs, Temporal adjusts automatically.

The JavaScriptDate objectcorrectly implements leap year rules, but calendar calculations involving leap years
can producesurprising resultsbecause invalid dates are automatically adjusted instead of reported as errors.

Temporal lets you choose what should happen when a date becomes invalid.

For example: February 29, 2024 + 1 year becomes invalid, because 2025 has no February 29.

Theoverflowoption controls what happens when a date calculation produces an invalid date.

Withoverflow: "constrain", Temporal changes the result to the nearest valid date.

Withoverflow: "reject", Temporal throws an error instead of changing the date.

